  "raw_html": "<button type='button' role='link' class='btn btn-link p-0 text-start' id='link_109' aria-label=\" Rajneesh Kumar & Anr. versus Ved Prakash pdf\"  class='noToken' href='#' onclick=javascript:open_pdf('109','2024','2024_11_1466_1471','2024INSC891'); ><font size='4'> <strong>RAJNEESH KUMAR & ANR.<span class='fst-italic'> versus </span>VED PRAKASH </strong>- <span class='escrText'>[2024] 11 S.C.R. 1466</span><span class='ncDisplay'>2024 INSC 891</span><input type='hidden' id='cnr' value=ESCR010007112024></button></font><br><strong>Coram : J.B. PARDIWALA, R MAHADEVAN</strong><br> Issue for Consideration Whether the litigant can be permitted to throw the entire blame on the head of the advocate for the delay in preferring appeal. Headnotes&dagger; Delay &ndash; Condonation of delay &ndash; The petitioners-plaintiffs herein were dissatisfied with the ex parte order passed in before the first appellate court by way of an appeal &ndash; However, the appeal was time barred by 534 days &ndash; First Appellate Court condoned the delay of 534 days in preferring appeal on the ground that the litigant should not suffer on account of negligence on the part of the advocate<br><strong class='caseDetailsTD' ><span style='color:#212F3D' > Decision Date :</span><font color='green'> 21-11-2024</font><span style='color:#212F3D' > | Case No :</span><font color='green'> SPECIAL LEAVE PETITION (CIVIL) No. 935/2021</font><span style='color:#212F3D' > | Disposal Nature :</span><font color='green'> Dismissed</font>   <span style='color:#212F3D' > |  Bench :</span><font color='green'> 2 Judges</font></strong><div class='row my-1 mt-2 border bg-light'><div class='col-auto'><a href='#' class='btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm my-1' onclick=open_splitview('109','2024','2024_11_1466_1471','2024INSC891','S')><i <i class='fas fa-columns me-2'></i>Split view</a></div><div class='col-auto'><a href='#' class='btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm my-1' onclick=open_splitview('109','2024','2024_1